 ## Overview
-Stream lines visualize 3D vector fields by tracing the trajectories of massless particles through the flow, solving the initial value problem defined by the velocity field [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. Unlike static glyphs that show direction at discrete points, stream lines reveal the continuous connectivity and topology of the flow, such as vortices and separation zones [S4, unofficial]. They are distinguished from path lines (which track particles in time-varying fields) by being tangential to the stationary flow field and generally not self-intersecting, except at cycles [S1, unofficial].
+Stream lines visualize 3D vector fields by tracing the trajectories of massless particles through the flow, solving the initial value problem defined by the velocity field [S3, S4, S5, unofficial]. They are distinguished from path lines (which track particles in time-varying fields) by being tangential to the stationary flow field and generally not self-intersecting, except at cycles [S3, unofficial]. Unlike static glyphs that show direction at discrete points, stream lines reveal continuous flow paths, such as vortices and turbulences observed in datasets like a delta wing [S6, unofficial].
 
